  "title": "Optional: komal and tivra",
  "summary": "The sargam names for the black keys.",
  "minutes": 6,
  "steps": [
    {
      "type": "explain",
      "title": "Komal",
      "body": "**Komal** means lowered: one key below the shuddha note. Re, Ga, Dha and Ni can each be komal. With Sa on C, komal Re is the black key between C and D.\n\n**Why it matters:** Many ragas and film songs use komal notes, and they are the sargam names for most black keys.",
      "exampleMidi": [
        61,
        63,
        68,
        70
      ],
      "labels": {
        "61": "komal Re",
        "63": "komal Ga",
        "68": "komal Dha",
        "70": "komal Ni"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "explain",
      "title": "Tivra",
      "body": "**Tivra** means raised: one key above. Only Ma can be tivra. With Sa on C, tivra Ma is F♯. Sa and Pa never change.\n\n**Why it matters:** Tivra Ma gives some ragas their bright, lifted sound. With komal and tivra, every key on the keyboard has a sargam name.",
      "exampleMidi": [
        65,
        66
      ],
      "labels": {
        "65": "Ma",
        "66": "tivra Ma"
      }
    },
